    "question": {
      ":question/id": "45ae9469-b078-57d8-bff2-369565a9d336",
      ":question/problem": "Find the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\left(2 x - 4\\right) e^{- y - 3}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\left(2 x - 4\\right) e^{- y - 3} \\\\ (e^{y + 3})\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2 x - 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (e^{y + 3})\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2 x - 4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (e^{y + 3})\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int (2 x - 4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (e^{y + 3})\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (2 x - 4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ e^{y + 3} & =\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\ne^{y + 3}=\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2}+C.\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
